            {
              "id": "d24537aa-c5dc-3cf1-854c-c5635cbf27b3",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen` function in C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string up to, but not including, the terminating null character. If the string does not have a null character, `strlen` will continue reading memory until it encounters one, which can lead to buffer overflows, memory corruption, and other security issues.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that strings are null-terminated before passing them to `strlen`. Also, consider using safer alternatives to `strlen`, such as `strnlen`, which takes a maximum length parameter and will not read past this length, even if it does not encounter a null character.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n\n// ...\n\nchar *ansi = /* ... */;\nsize_t length;\n\nif (ansi != NULL) {\n    length = strlen(ansi);\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, we first check if `ansi` is not `NULL` before passing it to `strlen`. This prevents `strlen` from reading memory it should not have access to.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<cstring>` library to use the `strlen` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "09c32bf9-56fd-3e77-85e0-35e72ebaab6a",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Found\" is a vulnerability that occurs when a program uses a function that can lead to a buffer overflow, which can lead to arbitrary code execution, denial-of-service, or information disclosure. In this case, the function `wcslen()` is used, which can lead to a buffer overflow if the string `str` is not null-terminated.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid using functions that do not check for buffer overflows. Instead, use safer alternatives that include bounds checking. In this case, you can use `wcsnlen()`, which takes an additional parameter specifying the maximum number of characters to consider.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cwchar>\n\n// ...\n\nsize_t max_chars = /* maximum number of characters that str can hold */;\nnum_chars = static_cast<int>(wcsnlen(str, max_chars));\n```\n\nIn this code, `wcsnlen()` is used instead of `wcslen()`. The `max_chars` parameter ensures that `wcsnlen()` does not read past the end of the buffer.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library to execute properly:\n\n- `<cwchar>`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are subject to change and may not be accessible in the future."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "e4243e86-30b7-3768-b2c1-2b1ae49b2503",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen` function in C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string until it reaches a null character (`\\0`). If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen` will continue reading memory until it encounters a null character, which can lead to buffer overflows, memory corruption, and other unexpected behavior.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `CloneString(c_str, strlen(c_str))`, there is a potential vulnerability if `c_str` is not null-terminated. This can lead to buffer overflow and other related issues.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that your strings are null-terminated. This can be done by initializing your strings with a null character or by manually adding a null character at the end of your strings.\n\nAlso, consider using safer alternatives to `strlen`, such as `strnlen`, which takes an additional parameter specifying the maximum number of characters to count. This can prevent buffer overflows by ensuring that `strnlen` does not read past the end of your buffer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer version of the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n\n// ...\n\nsize_t len = strnlen(c_str, MAX_LEN);\nCloneString(c_str, len);\n```\n\nIn this version, `strnlen` is used instead of `strlen`, and `MAX_LEN` is the maximum number of characters that `strnlen` will count. This prevents `strnlen` from reading past the end of the buffer if `c_str` is not null-terminated.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<cstring>` library for the `strnlen` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "60f283bb-889c-3fb4-aa5f-41f85bbdcb1d",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnsecured URL Vulnerability refers to the security risk associated with the use of unsecured URLs in a C++ program. This vulnerability can lead to various security issues such as data leakage, unauthorized access, and even remote code execution. The specific vulnerability sink in this case is the URL \"http://caffe.berkeleyvision.org\". This URL is unsecured because it uses the HTTP protocol instead of the more secure HTTPS protocol. This means that any data sent to or received from this URL is not encrypted and can be intercepted by attackers.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use secure URLs (HTTPS) instead of unsecured ones (HTTP). HTTPS ensures that the data sent between the client and the server is encrypted and cannot be intercepted. Also, validate all URLs and user inputs to prevent attacks such as URL injection and cross-site scripting (XSS).\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the unsecured URL with a secured one. If the server supports HTTPS, simply change \"http\" to \"https\". If not, you may need to find a secure alternative to the server. Here is an example of how to fix this in C++:\n\n```cpp\n// Vulnerable code\nstd::string url = \"http://caffe.berkeleyvision.org\";\n\n// Fixed code\nstd::string url = \"https://caffe.berkeleyvision.org\";\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe specific library dependencies required by the code example will depend on the rest of the code. However, in general, to work with URLs in C++, you may need libraries such as `libcurl` or `Boost.Asio`.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n- [OWASP Top 10 2017: A6-Security Misconfiguration](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A6_2017-Security_Misconfiguration)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)\n- [CWE-601: URL Redirection to Untrusted Site ('Open Redirect')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/601.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS137138"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c1e74217-661e-3b9a-b1f4-3a3a900df21f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnsecured URL Vulnerability refers to the security risk associated with the use of unsecured URLs in a C++ program. This vulnerability can lead to various security issues such as data leakage, unauthorized access, and even remote code execution. The specific vulnerability sink in this case is the URL \"http://caffe.berkeleyvision.org\". This URL is unsecured because it uses the HTTP protocol instead of the more secure HTTPS protocol. This means that any data sent to or received from this URL is not encrypted and can be intercepted by attackers.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use secure URLs (HTTPS) instead of unsecured ones (HTTP). HTTPS ensures that the data sent between the client and the server is encrypted and cannot be intercepted. Also, validate all URLs and user inputs to prevent attacks such as URL injection and cross-site scripting (XSS).\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the unsecured URL with a secured one. If the server supports HTTPS, simply change \"http\" to \"https\". If not, you may need to find a secure alternative to the server. Here is an example of how to fix this in C++:\n\n```cpp\n// Vulnerable code\nstd::string url = \"http://caffe.berkeleyvision.org\";\n\n// Fixed code\nstd::string url = \"https://caffe.berkeleyvision.org\";\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe specific library dependencies required by the code example will depend on the rest of the code. However, in general, to work with URLs in C++, you may need libraries such as `libcurl` or `Boost.Asio`.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n- [OWASP Top 10 2017: A6-Security Misconfiguration](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A6_2017-Security_Misconfiguration)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)\n- [CWE-601: URL Redirection to Untrusted Site ('Open Redirect')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/601.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS137138"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a0adee32-f46b-3e4d-8218-cafc55f44078",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ programming language refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other problems. These functions are often prohibited in secure coding standards. One such function is `fprintf`.\n\nThe `fprintf` function can lead to vulnerabilities if not used correctly. It can cause format string vulnerabilities if the format string is not a constant, or if it's composed dynamically, it can lead to buffer overflows if the output is larger than the buffer.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to `fprintf` such as `snprintf` or `vsnprintf`. These functions include the size of the buffer as an argument, which can prevent buffer overflows.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `fprintf`, use `snprintf` or `vsnprintf`. Here is an example:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdio.h>\n\nvoid safe_function(const char* format, ...) {\n    char buffer[1024];\n    va_list args;\n    va_start(args, format);\n    vsnprintf(buffer, sizeof(buffer), format, args);\n    va_end(args);\n    // Now buffer contains the formatted string.\n    // You can write it to file with fwrite or similar\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- stdio.h\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n\n## CWE\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "705e699f-3ac8-381c-8acb-f1e595b40f9c",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, the vulnerability \"Avoid Using Non-Cryptographic or Weak Random Number Generators\" refers to the use of random number generators that are not cryptographically secure. This can lead to predictable randomness which can be exploited by an attacker to predict the outcome of the random number generator. This can lead to a variety of security issues, such as predictable session identifiers, predictable password reset tokens, or predictable cryptographic keys.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the `shuffle` function is used with a non-cryptographic random number generator (`prefetch_rng`), which can lead to the aforementioned security issues.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use a cryptographically secure random number generator. In C++, this can be achieved by using the `random_device` class from the `<random>` library. This class uses a hardware device to generate random numbers, which are cryptographically secure.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::random_device rd;\nstd::mt19937 g(rd());\n\nshuffle(lines_.begin(), lines_.end(), g);\n```\n\nIn this fixed version, a `random_device` is used to seed a Mersenne Twister engine (`mt19937`), which is then used as the random number generator for the `shuffle` function.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe fixed code snippet requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<random>`: for `std::random_device` and `std::mt19937`\n- `<algorithm>`: for `std::shuffle`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS148264"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d0b1f53d-bee0-3753-b41b-cce5ff25ec38",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, the vulnerability \"Avoid Using Non-Cryptographic or Weak Random Number Generators\" refers to the use of random number generators that are not cryptographically secure. This can lead to predictable values being generated, which can be exploited by an attacker to predict future values and compromise the security of the system.\n\nThe specific vulnerability sink in the code `this->layer_param_.image_data_param().shuffle()` could potentially use a weak or non-cryptographic random number generator for the shuffle operation, making the shuffled data predictable.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use a cryptographically secure random number generator. In C++, this can be achieved using the `<random>` library, which provides a variety of random number generators that are suitable for cryptographic use.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix using the `<random>` library:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n#include <algorithm>\n\n// ...\n\nstd::vector<int> indices(this->layer_param_.image_data_param().size());\nstd::iota(indices.begin(), indices.end(), 0);\n\nstd::random_device rd;\nstd::mt19937 g(rd());\n\nstd::shuffle(indices.begin(), indices.end(), g);\n\n// Use shuffled indices...\n```\n\nIn this code, a `std::random_device` is used to seed a Mersenne Twister (`std::mt19937`), which is a cryptographically secure random number generator. The `std::shuffle` function is then used with this generator to shuffle the indices.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<random>`: For the random number generator and distribution.\n- `<algorithm>`: For the `std::shuffle` function.\n- `<numeric>`: For the `std::iota` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS148264"
                ]
