            {
              "id": "309a345f-11fd-3e70-97a8-3c9dfced0404",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnsecured URL Vulnerability in C++ is a security flaw that occurs when a program does not validate or incorrectly validates an URL before loading it. This can lead to various security issues such as information disclosure, denial of service, or remote code execution if the URL is used to load data from a remote server or to launch a process.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n1. Always validate URLs before using them. This includes checking that the URL is using a secure protocol (https), and that it points to a trusted and expected domain.\n\n2. Use libraries or functions that automatically handle URL validation and sanitization.\n\n3. Avoid using user-supplied data to form URLs without proper validation and sanitization.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe following is a simple example of how to validate a URL using the `Poco::URI` class from the Poco C++ Libraries:\n\n```cpp\n#include <Poco/URI.h>\n\nvoid loadURL(const std::string& url) {\n    Poco::URI uri;\n    try {\n        uri = Poco::URI(url);\n    } catch (Poco::Exception& e) {\n        // Handle exception\n        return;\n    }\n\n    if (uri.getScheme() != \"https\") {\n        // Handle non-https URL\n        return;\n    }\n\n    // Continue with loading the URL\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe above code requires the Poco C++ Libraries.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Unvalidated Redirects and Forwards Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Unvalidated_Redirects_and_Forwards_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-601: URL Redirection to Untrusted Site ('Open Redirect')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/601.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS137138"
                ]
              }
